Student Spending & Expense Behavior Analysis (Power BI)

Problem Statement
This project analyzes how students allocate their monthly expenses and identifies patterns in financial behavior across different demographic and behavioral factors such as income, impulse purchasing, and expense tracking habits.

The objective is to help students improve financial decision-making and assist educational institutions in promoting financial awareness.

Objectives
- Understand student spending distribution across categories
- Analyze relationship between income and spending
- Study impact of impulse purchasing on expenses
- Evaluate effect of financial awareness on savings
- Identify behavioral patterns in financial decision-making

Dataset Description
- Sample Size: 131 student responses
- Data Type: Survey-based primary data

Variables:
- Income, Age, Total Spending, Savings
- Gender, Living Arrangement
- Expense Tracking, Impulse Purchase
- Spending categories: Food, Transport, Shopping, Entertainment, Academic



Data Transformation (Power Query)
- Removed unnecessary columns (e.g., email)
- Cleaned and standardized data
- Created calculated fields:
- Total Spending
- Savings
- Financial Status
- Applied unpivoting for category-wise analysis

DAX Measures Used
- Total Students
- Average Income
- Average Spending
- Savings Rate
- Spending Ratio
- Savers % / Overspenders %
- Financial Awareness Score

Dashboard Highlights

Page 1: Financial Overview
- Spending by Category (Food highest)
- Financial Status Distribution
- Gender-based Spending Comparison
- Living Arrangement Impact

Page 2: Behavioral Insights
- Income vs Spending Relationship
- Impulse Purchase Impact
- Expense Tracking vs Savings
- Financial Awareness vs Savings
- Spending Trend Over Time

Key Insights
- Food is the highest expense category
- Income and spending show strong positive correlation
- Impulse buyers spend significantly more
- Financial awareness improves savings behavior
- PG students spend more than hostel students
- Expense tracking alone does not guarantee savings

Recommendations
- Improve financial literacy programs for students
- Encourage controlled impulse spending
- Promote structured budgeting habits
- Focus on financial awareness training

Conclusion
Student financial behavior is influenced by income, lifestyle, and behavioral patterns. Financial awareness and disciplined spending habits play a key role in improving savings and financial stability.


